{"title":"Knapsack With One Weight Change","difficulty":"Hard","statement":"This is the classic 0/1 knapsack with one twist: you are allowed to **change the weight of at most one item**.\n\nThere are `n` items. Item `i` has a profit `profit[i]` and a normal weight `weight[i]`. It also has an alternative weight `alt_weight[i]` that it takes instead if you choose it as the one item whose weight is changed (the alternative may be smaller or larger than the normal weight). You may pick **at most one** item to change; every other item keeps its normal weight. Each item can be taken at most once, and the total weight of the items you take must not exceed the capacity `W`.\n\nChanging an item that you do not end up taking has no effect, so \"at most one change\" simply means you may use one alternative weight among the items you take, or none.\n\nReturn the maximum total profit you can collect.\n\n**Follow-up:** the question was reported as \"you can change the weight of at most one item, then find the maximum profit\", with the hint that a 3-dimensional DP is needed. Can you explain why one extra dimension (changed or not) is enough, and reduce the memory to O(W)?","inputFormat":"The first line contains two integers `n` and `W`.\nEach of the next `n` lines contains three integers `weight[i]`, `alt_weight[i]` and `profit[i]`.","outputFormat":"Print a single integer: the maximum total profit.","constraints":["1 ≤ n ≤ 200","1 ≤ W ≤ 5000","1 ≤ weight[i], alt_weight[i] ≤ 1000","1 ≤ profit[i] ≤ 10^6"],"examples":[{"input":"3 6\n5 2 10\n4 4 6\n3 3 4","output":"16","explanation":"Without a change the best you can do is the first item alone for 10. Changing the first item's weight from 5 to 2 lets you take items 0 and 1 for a total weight of 2 + 4 = 6 and profit 16."},{"input":"2 5\n3 6 4\n2 9 3","output":"7","explanation":"Both alternative weights are larger, so changing nothing is best: 3 + 2 = 5 fits exactly, for profit 4 + 3 = 7."},{"input":"2 3\n3 1 5\n3 1 5","output":"5","explanation":"Only one weight may change. With one item at weight 1 and the other at weight 3 the total is 4 > 3, so only a single item fits and the profit is 5. Changing both would allow 10, but that is not permitted."}],"tests":[{"input":"1 1\n1 1 1","output":"1"},{"input":"1 1\n2 1 5","output":"5"},{"input":"1 1\n2 3 5","output":"0"},{"input":"1 5\n6 2 7","output":"7"},{"input":"3 10\n4 4 3\n5 5 4\n6 6 5","output":"8"},{"input":"2 15\n12 11 27\n6 4 16","output":"27"},{"input":"2 2\n11 8 3\n3 1 11","output":"11"},{"input":"2 17\n4 2 8\n7 2 18","output":"26"},{"input":"3 8\n11 11 17\n4 3 2\n1 3 4","output":"6"},{"input":"3 9\n8 7 9\n4 11 15\n12 12 3","output":"15"},{"input":"3 11\n4 2 23\n10 10 2\n9 4 15","output":"38"},{"input":"4 7\n8 6 6\n5 10 20\n3 2 30\n1 6 4","output":"50"},{"input":"4 7\n11 2 13\n12 4 1\n9 12 13\n9 11 17","output":"13"},{"input":"4 13\n10 3 22\n3 7 13\n4 12 21\n3 5 26","output":"82"},{"input":"5 16\n6 8 14\n8 8 8\n4 1 28\n1 9 7\n2 12 25","output":"74"},{"input":"5 18\n9 3 28\n6 3 2\n2 11 30\n11 3 15\n9 8 19","output":"77"},{"input":"5 6\n5 1 27\n12 11 12\n5 5 22\n8 11 15\n9 12 8","output":"49"},{"input":"6 13\n8 6 8\n8 11 3\n5 8 16\n2 11 3\n7 9 24\n7 10 13","output":"40"},{"input":"6 1\n4 8 29\n3 10 1\n7 5 16\n11 8 13\n1 7 11\n1 9 10","output":"11"},{"input":"6 14\n9 10 28\n8 10 5\n2 4 21\n1 4 7\n6 6 25\n9 4 2","output":"56"},{"input":"7 17\n12 11 12\n12 7 4\n3 9 10\n2 3 10\n11 8 6\n6 11 15\n11 6 2","output":"37"},{"input":"7 7\n12 11 16\n7 12 7\n2 8 9\n7 6 24\n8 1 14\n6 9 6\n7 7 6","output":"24"},{"input":"7 5\n2 2 17\n10 2 1\n1 9 8\n3 10 19\n5 11 12\n2 12 4\n11 5 1","output":"36"},{"input":"8 11\n2 3 2\n7 1 24\n11 12 11\n11 12 17\n2 12 22\n4 4 11\n2 7 9\n2 7 28","output":"94"},{"input":"8 13\n1 7 20\n3 1 4\n6 3 9\n12 1 12\n10 10 27\n5 7 20\n7 4 26\n10 9 4","output":"70"},{"input":"8 12\n5 10 24\n12 4 3\n2 6 10\n11 5 6\n3 3 30\n11 2 13\n9 7 4\n9 5 11","output":"77"},{"input":"9 20\n5 10 13\n6 12 25\n5 8 7\n2 10 30\n3 9 9\n7 3 22\n10 5 12\n12 